def arbitration_losses(ids, width=11):
    """Simulate CAN arbitration among distinct identifiers that start together.
    Return {id: position at which it lost (0 = first, most significant bit), or None for the winner}."""
    sending = set(ids)
    result = {i: None for i in ids}
    for pos in range(width):
        bit = width - 1 - pos
        bus = min((i >> bit) & 1 for i in sending)        # wired-AND: any 0 makes the bus 0
        for i in list(sending):
            if (i >> bit) & 1 == 1 and bus == 0:
                result[i] = pos
                sending.discard(i)
    return result

exercises.solution:
```python
def arbitration_losses(ids, width=11):
    sending = set(ids)
    result = {i: None for i in ids}
    for pos in range(width):
        bit = width - 1 - pos
        bus = min((i >> bit) & 1 for i in sending)
        for i in list(sending):
            if (i >> bit) & 1 == 1 and bus == 0:
                result[i] = pos
                sending.discard(i)
    return result
```

For the lesson's four nodes, `0x320` loses at position 1, `0x1A0` at 3, `0x125` at 8, and `0x123` wins. Several nodes can lose at the same position (`0x402` and `0x403` both lose at position 9 to `0x400` and `0x401`). The winner is always the smallest identifier, because at the first position where the survivors differ, the ones with a 1 there are larger and drop out.
